On Feb 21, 9:56 pm, ais523 <ais...@nethack4.org> wrote:
> wumpus wrote:
> The aim is to ensure that any user of the software will
> be able to change it and continue using the changed
> version, and distribute their changed version for free if
> they want to.

That's part of it, yes, but it's more than that.  The goal,
ultimately, is for there not to be ANY software that the
user can't do this with.  The GPL is designed not just to
protect users of the specific piece of software licensed
under it, but to create a situation where effectively all
software must be compatible with the GPL in order to be
useful to anyone.  That's almost certainly unachievable,
but that's the utopia the FSF is working toward.

> This is pretty much exactly contrary to Apple's aims

Well, yes.  Apple is a vendor of proprietary systems.  The
FSF would strongly prefer that Apple not exist, or at least
not be in the line of business they are in.  (Apple was not
the original bugaboo.  Outfits like DEC and Sun were the
original bugaboo.  Apple is a Johnny come lately.  But the
principles involved haven't changed.)

> I'm glad that NetHack has this sort of license, really

Personally I tend to prefer permissive licenses, but
this is not very important to me.  Any license that
doesn't prevent me from tinkering and customizing
is fundamentally adequate for my needs.

Software that *I* write tends to get released into
the public domain.

> (although its license is a very old and buggy version of
> the GPL; the most annoying bug is its complete
> incompatibility with other licenses even if they have
> very similar spirits).

All versions of the GPL have this issue.  (That's why the
"or any later version" clause has to exist.  If it didn't,
the GPL would be incompatible with later versions of
itself, if they make any actual changes at all.)  It's a
consequence of the way the GPL is using copyright
protection, in a very different spirit from how it was
originally intended.  Copyright law isn't designed to
make things like the GPL possible, let alone practical.

The GPL is *clever*, I will give it that.  It puts anyone
who makes a proprietary version of GPLed software into the
awkward position of attempting to argue that they had a
valid license that still applied, when it is clear they
were violating the terms of the license.

> I got interested in NetHack, started writing patches, and
> now I'm maintaining a variant. And even though the
> DevTeam are not putting out code, and thus not useful to
> anyone any more for continuing the game, the community
> can still continue it precisely because I've got that
> right to change it.

That would be equally true if it were under a permissive
license.

> An iPhone version of NetHack wouldn't allow this. Sure,

With a permissive license, the creator of the iPhone
version could distribute his (pre-App-Store-packaged) code
separately, if he chose, and anyone could do with it more
or less whatever they wanted.  (Yes, it would ALSO be
possible for someone to create a proprietary version.
Guess which version we'd be more interested in?)

> I'd probably be able to get hold of the source. But I
> wouldn't be able to test any patches I wrote for it,
> because I wouldn't be able to put them on my iPhone.

That's a problem with the iPhone itself.

You'd need the official dev kit (or developer account or
whatever they call it) and whatever else an App Store
developer needs in order to run test versions.  The only
other way to run custom software on it is to jailbreak it,
which at minimum voids your warranty and in some
jurisdictions may be illegal.  That's true for any app, no
matter what license it's under, because the app license
isn't the problem here.  iOS is inherently problematic for
people who want to run custom versions of things without
paying extra for the privilege.

Another way to look at it is that the cost of the developer
account and stuff is effectively a mandatory part of the
cost of the device, if you want a device on which you can
run custom anything.

Since iOS is about as customizable as a brick wall in any
case (you can, umm, apply a coat of paint and change the
color of the surface, if you want), I personally am highly
uninterested in it in any case.

> And I wouldn't be able to share what I'd done with other
> people, even if I did it, due to the App Store's
> restrictions;

Actually, with a permissive license, you could package up a
ready-to-use version and put that in the App Store, and
then someone else who makes additional changes could also
package up a ready-to-use version for the App Store.
(Apple would have to *approve* it, of course, but that's
true for everything in the App Store.)  Users who wanted to
*modify* it would have to get the code from you through
some other channel (e.g., download it from your website),
and they'd also need the dev kit and whatnot to be able to
use their modifications, but that's an inherent limitation
of the iOS/App Store ecosystem.

Come to that, even if you can't put the thing in the App
Store, you *can* still share it with other people, but
everyone who wants to run it (even if they don't care about
modifying it) will need the dev kit (or a jailbreak), so
they can run something that doesn't come from the App
Store.  So it'd be of no use to end users, only developers.


